s that our analyses can  contribute to justice is when were  invited to testify in court cases has  happened in 2002 with my colleague and  HR dad co-founder dr. Patrick ball when  he testified against slobodan milosevic  at the International Criminal Tribunal  for the former Yugoslavia what he's  presenting here are patterns of refugee  flow as people fled across the border  into Albania and also patterns of known  killings that occurred during that  conflict he also testified in 2013 this  time in Guatemala against general efraín  ríos Montt who was the de facto leader  of Guatemala in the 1980s and was  charged with acts of genocide the  analyses that we presented in that case  were a comparison of the relative risk  of death for members of the indigenous  population as compared to the relative  risk of death for members of the  non-indigenous population and the wide  disparity in those taint in those  differences in relative risks the  lawyers argued were consistent with  patterns you would expect to see if acts  of genocide were occurring no Rios Montt  was found guilty but that verdict only  stood for 10 days the Constitutional  Court overturned the verdict on a legal  technicality there are efforts right now  to restart that trial but there is a  long and winding legal road ahead of  those efforts most recently last year  Patrick testified again against his  Shane hob ray the former leader of Chad  in the 1980s this time Patrick presented  crude mortality rates for prisoners in  prisons run by secret police under the  command of his Shane hob ray now this  particular story has a slightly better  coda  just a few months ago in May of this  year his Shane Hari was found guilty and  sentenced to life in prison so those are  some of the biggest most visible  outcomes of some of our work but what's  the actual analytical work that we're  presenting in those cases well we rely  primarily on two classes of methods one  of which is record linkage or database  deduplication  and just to get a sense who is already  familiar with record linkage record  linkage problems okay so this crops up  in a wide variety of applications  essentially it involves needing to  identify when multiple records refer to  the same individual or event or entity  and it'll talk about how that crops up  in our specific work but this is an  incomplete list of the references that  have influenced our thinking and our  work in this particular area and perhaps  most applicable for this audience of  course is that we rely very heavily on  syfy numpy and SK learn to do this  particular piece of our work  the other main analytical method that we  use is a class of statistical tools  called multiple systems estimation or  sometimes where it was developed in  ecology it's referred to as capture  recapture this is a broad class of tools  that's been developed in everything from  ecology to demography to statistics to  public health research and now to human  rights work again this is an incomplete  list of the references that have  influenced our thinking on this work  primarily we implement our MSC work in a  variety of our packages given the title  of this conference I'm gonna focus this  morning primarily on the work we do in  Python but if folks want to talk about  art as well I'm happy to get into that  during QA or offline afterwards so to  get into this morning's particular  example this project started as so many  of our projects do when a partner  approached us with some data and a  question what happened was in 2012 the  United Nations office of the High  Commissioner for Human Rights came to us  and at the time they had access to four  different lists of named victims who had  been killed in the ongoing conflict in  Syria and they came to us and they said  this is a little bit different from the  kinds of situations we usually work in  we don't usually have multiple sources  and we're not really sure how to make  heads or tails of this information and  