s of their  community or the same victim may be  reported to multiple sources perhaps  each of the sources have access to the  same original information be that a  religious leader or a morgue record or  hospital records so this is the  aggregated total number of named victims  across all four of these sources and  this is why we start with a record  linkage problem because we need to  identify those multiple records that  refer to the same individual and for  this morning the records that I'm  talking about cover the period from the  beginning of the conflict through the  end of last year so when I talk about  sources and records what do I mean what  does the data actually look like in this  case it literally is a list of named  victims this is a screenshot from the  violations documentation Center this is  a public-facing website that they  maintain so anyone has access to this  information that's part of their  advocacy efforts they believe it's  important to make this information  available to draw attention to the  conflict and tell the story of the  victims and all four of the groups  record very similar information the name  of the victim the date and location  where they were killed and then  occasionally we get other demographic  information in most cases we know their  sex rarely do we know their age although  the groups do try and record that  information and a few other bits of  demographic information so like I said  this becomes a record linkage problem  because the first thing we need to do is  to identify the total number of uniquely  identified victims who are described by  these four sources so this diagram  describes the way our team thinks of  this record linkage problem and if  you're familiar with this class of  methods specifically what we implement  is supervised record linkage so the  outside of the circle everything that  talks about human labeling and starts  with a TS  that's where we send records out to  humans and ask them to review it 