ilable on our website it's in a  corner of the website we're developing  called the tech corner where we're  starting to include more of these  technical details of some of our work  and it's all written in Jupiter  notebooks and includes code snippets so  you can work through all of those  details if you're curious about them but  that's the blocking step so now I'm  gonna focus primarily on the next three  so if we think that we've finished that  first step we miraculously somehow have  a number of comparisons that's  manageable now we need to numerically  summarize how similar or dissimilar each  of those pairs of Records are so we need  to create some feature vectors that  describe for example the number of days  apart between the recorded dates of  death whether the location of those  deaths is geographically nearby and then  most of the action of course is in the  names so we want to do some typical  things we want to look at edit distance  between the names we use levenshtein and  djaro implementations in python we want  to look at the phonetic similarities of  the names we use a metaphore  implementation in python we want to look  at the name tokens we want to see how  similar in a variety of ways all of  these records are so we end up at the  end of the day with these feature  vectors for each pair that we consider  and that summarizes how similar or  different each pair is to each other and  now we need to make a decision are those  pairs in fact the same individual person  and to do that we need a classification  model now fortunately for us SK learn  has basically any classification model  you might ever want to consider and it  also has baked in all of these really  useful metrics for evaluating those  classification models and I also have to  say that the documentation for SK learn  is so useful for carrying out that  evaluation for making decisions  about how your classification models are  performing and so here we're just trying  out a few different classification  models trying to make some decisions and  what we're feeding into these models are  those feature vectors the numerical  summaries of how similar or dissimilar  each pair are and the labeled data or  the training data that we've generated  by sending pairs of records out to  humans and asking them to label them yes  they refer to the same individual no  they don't refer to the same individual  and in the literature this role is  actually referred to as an Oracle which  i think is possibly the best job title  ever so we have a couple of Oracle's go  out label a subset of the data and then  that's what we're feeding into these  classification models so in this case  we're trying a random forest a decision  tree and logistic regression and wolf  the random forest is killing us but the  what we're feeding that model is the X  are those comparison vectors or feature  vectors and the Y are the labels from  the oracle reviews and then vectors is  just the pandas dataframe that that data  lives in and then what we're going to  get out of each of these models is  technically a score I think of it as a  probability depending upon what model  you're fitting it may or may not truly  be a probability but essentially it is a  way to make a decision about whether  those two records refer to the same  individual or not so you use the metrics  that are baked into a scaler and you  evaluate your different model  possibilities and ultimately you make  some kind of a decision in our case we  actually ended up going with X G boost  but that's how you classify records as  referring to the same individual or not  but now we only have considered pairwise  comparisons and now we need to move to  clustering because if I decide based on  the score that I get out of a  classification model  that record a and record B are the same  person and record B and record C are the  same person is it the right thing to do  to transitively close those  relationships and say records a B and C  are a cluster of three that all describe  the same person well that's where we  need to start and so again this is where  we turn to Network X and then a class of  functions that are inside PI cluster  hierarchy so the first thing that we're  doing here is we're just reading in  those classified pairs and I included  this little code snippet mostly because  I wanted to highlight we've read in over  44 million pairs so we're not quite to  Big Data yet we can pass that around in  memory but it's a big enough file that  we need to be really thoughtful about  any manipulations we're going to do to  it any sort of passing back and forth  that we're going to do and these 44  million classified pairs are what come  out of that first blocking step if we  didn't go through that complication of  blocking we would have billions with a B  pairs so just getting down to something  that we can handle in memory requires  that first step but fortunately for us  we do get down to something that is very  manageable so we read in all of these  pairs that now have scores associated  with them from our classification model  and now we're going to group them into  clusters so we're going to use Network X  to draw a graph we're gonna set a  somewhat arbitrary threshold because  it's something that we're gonna  experiment with moving around on and  we're going to use that threshold to say  any pair that has a score above that  threshold we're going to declare a match  we're gonna say that pair of records  refers to the same individual and we're  going to use those  positive pairs to add edges to our graph  and then from there we can generate  these connected components with this  which essentially are our clusters they  do that transitive closure they say if  these pairs match and these other pairs  match bring them together into a cluster  which is a pretty reasonable place to  start but if you we look at those  connected components and we look at the  size of the clusters they generate  largely they generate fairly reasonable  clusters of small sizes but then we have  a few out there at the tail that are  very big and in fact in this particular  case we have one cluster that contains  over 12,000 records and I don't believe  that's true I don't believe one person  was reported to these sources 12,000  times and that's the weakness of  transitive closure is that sometimes you  have a few records that appear to be  similar to a lot of other records and  you need to go break those back up and  the best way that we've found to do that  is using hierarchical agglomerative  clustering which again thank goodness  baked into SK learn or baked into sci-fi  my apologies but it turns out that it's  very slow to run and that it's very  complicated to get to run over all of  these different connected components and  so again this is one of those  complicated problems that Patrick ended  up writing up for us and our tech corner  and I really enjoy in this particular  write-up he describes how he  enthusiastically said about solving the  wrong problem for a few days I imagine  all of us can sympathize with that and  so he highlights sort of going through  the process of recognizing what the  right problem was which in this  particular case was that there were  multiple different ways to implement the  hierarchical agglomerative clustering  algorithm and he needed two different  ways because one way was faster for  smaller clusters and one way was faster  for bigger clusters and so he needed a  mixed solution  and so this blog post again sort of runs  through all of those technical details  that's another Jupiter notebook code  snippets the usual kinds of things one  other thing that I just want to  highlight again from that class of  functions in scifi clustering hierarchy  is a function called dendrograms and we  found this really useful because as we  were going through this process and as  we were evaluating the results that we  were getting we were noticing that when  we send pairs to the oracles and we ask  them yes or no do these refer to the  same individual they had a tendency to  over match which isn't all that  surprising because if all you had to  look at are two records in isolation and  the records are a little bit different  but kind of similar you might be  inclined to say yes those are probably a  match but if instead you see those two  pairs as part of a broader cluster like  this that all have some similarities and  some differences then it gets easier to  identify oh those those two that looked  similar in fact belong two separate  clusters that describe different  distinct individual victims and so what  we started to do in the iterative  process that we work through where we  fit our classification models and we run  our algorithms and we cluster records  together but then we also send things  back to the Oracle for review we develop  more training data and iterate until we  think we've arrived at the right answer  is that sending our Oracle records that  looked like this and in fact including  the dendrogram visualization really  helped them to identify how many unique  individuals are in fact described by  this cluster of Records and then they  could advise us on where that threshold  for scores should be set where should we  set a cut point to actually break apart  these clusters in a way that makes sense  so we really rely on a variety of the  tools in SK learn and inside PI for a  number of steps in this record linkage  work so that brings us to the end of  clustering that's the bulk of this  record linkage work there's a little bit  of merging and post-processing and then  of course we iterate through this  pipeline a lot but the end of the record  linkage piece of the work gets us to the  number of uniquely identified victims  who've been killed in Syria and our  substantive question is how many people  have been killed in Syria which is not  the same question and in fact I've  slightly missed titled my talk this  morning because there's this whole other  step that we have to take using multiple  systems estimation to build from what we  know and can observe to estimate what we  don't know to actually be able to answer  this question but that's a whole nother  talk so I want to wrap up this morning  with a quote from Patrick then I think  really describes the motivation behind  the work that we do because we have a  tendency to really get down into the  weeds of the technical aspects of the  work and the scientific methods and we  are a team of scientists we find all of  those components of our work very  interesting but the reason we dive so  deeply into those technical details is  because at HR dag we believe we are  morally obligated to do the best work  that's technically possible so that the  victims story is heard and believed to  do otherwise disrespects the victims  suffering so I want to say thank you  again to this community for helping us  to do the best work that's technically  possible thank you  I think we have plenty of time for  questions yes  oh great talk thank you I guess my  question is if I'm the defense attorney  for example and and the prosecution is  presenting this kind of evidence isn't I  mean if I'm defending can't I just poke  holes and sort of the entire methodology  and say that you know the data is  sampled wrong or it's doesn't you know I  mean it seems like when you absorb more  complexity in the analysis you've you've  created little entry points for other  kinds of arguments is that does that  happen or yes that's definitely true you  could certainly try and those are  lessons that we're learning over the  course of testifying in these cases in  2002 unfortunately a version of that  basically happened where it wasn't so  much that holes were poked in our  analyses it was that the lawyers and the  judges felt they couldn't evaluate the  strength of the analyses and so they  just chose to disregard them and so  we've been working on I want to say not  so much I mean we always want to present  the most rigorous analyses that we can  and fortunately with these two methods  we have sort of the full strength of the  scientific community kind of backing up  a lot of the choices that we're making  but what we have been really working on  is how can we present such complex  analyses in a way that the courtroom  gets it and feels that it's useful and I  can only say that in the Rios Montt  trial we felt like we had succeeded  because the judge specifically  referenced our work in her findings and  said that it confirmed and amplified the  witness accounts from individuals but it  definitely is an ongoing battle that  we're working on is how to present this  work more clearly and how to develop  more relationships within the legal  system to to develop sets of rules and  standards for evaluating this kind of  evidence in court cases  is I what jurisdictions do these  typically have I'm and I I know some of  them are under ICC but I said you know a  variety of jurisdictional concerns and  how two different courts consider this  kind of evidence differently should I  say the second one that's completely  unrelated now or should I wait for a  second um I think wait for a second  because that's that's a great question  and it is a variety of jurisdictions so  in the Rios Montt case that was a local  Guatemalan courtroom and so that they  have their own sort of jurisdictional  and evidentiary rules and then right  with the International Criminal Tribunal  for the former Yugoslavia was before the  ICC but it also but it also had its own  sort of special tribunal sets so yes  each of these have their own different  jurisdiction and evidentiary rules that  that we have to work with prosecutors to  sort of figure out and understand  mystical data techniques not many of  them are formalized specifically about  statistical data techniques the ICC is  working on that and we're trying to  advise them a