cation  and just to get a sense who is already  familiar with record linkage record  linkage problems okay so this crops up  in a wide variety of applications  essentially it involves needing to  identify when multiple records refer to  the same individual or event or entity  and it'll talk about how that crops up  in our specific work but this is an  incomplete list of the references that  have influenced our thinking and our  work in this particular area and perhaps  most applicable for this audience of  course is that we rely very heavily on  syfy numpy and SK learn to do this  particular piece of our work  the other main analytical method that we  use is a class of statistical tools  called multiple systems estimation or  sometimes where it was developed in  ecology it's referred to as capture  recapture this is a broad class of tools  that's been developed in everything from  ecology to demography to statistics to  public health research and now to human  rights work again this is an incomplete  list of the references that have  influenced our thinking on this work  primarily we implement our MSC work in a  variety of our packages given the title  of this conference I'm gonna focus this  morning primarily on the work we do in  Python but if folks want to talk about  art as well I'm happy to get into that  during QA or offline afterwards so to  get into this morning's particular  example this project started as so many  of our projects do when a partner  approached us with some data and a  question what happened was in 2012 the  United Nations office of the High  Commissioner for Human Rights came to us  and at the time they had access to four  different lists of named victims who had  been killed in the ongoing conflict in  Syria and they came to us and they said  this is a little bit different from the  kinds of situations we usually work in  we don't usually have multiple sources  and we're not really sure how to make  heads or tails of this information and  