ilable on our website it's in a  corner of the website we're developing  called the tech corner where we're  starting to include more of these  technical details of some of our work  and it's all written in Jupiter  notebooks and includes code snippets so  you can work through all of those  details if you're curious about them but  that's the blocking step so now I'm  gonna focus primarily on the next three  so if we think that we've finished that  first step we miraculously somehow have  a number of comparisons that's  manageable now we need to numerically  summarize how similar or dissimilar each  of those pairs of Records are so we need  to create some feature vectors that  describe for example the number of days  apart between the recorded dates of  death whether the location of those  deaths is geographically nearby and then  most of the action of course is in the  names so we want to do some typical  things we want to look at edit distance  between the names we use levenshtein and  djaro implementations in python we want  to look at the phonetic similarities of  the names we use a metaphore  implementation in python we want to look  at the name tokens we want to see how  similar in a variety of ways all of  these records are so we end up at the  end of the day with these feature  vectors for each pair that we consider  and that summarizes how similar or  different each pair is to each other and  now we need to make a decision are those  pairs in fact the same individual person  and to do that we need a classification  model now fortunately for us SK learn  has basically any classification model  you might ever want to consider and it  also has baked in all of these really  useful metrics for evaluating those  classification models and I also have to  say that the documentation for SK learn  is so useful for carrying out that  evaluation for making decisions  about how your classification models are  performing and so here we're just trying  out a few different